<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Holland</h1><p class="st-meta">880 CE – 1581 CE (approx.) · capital The Hague</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The coastal county that grew from Frisian marshland into the richest province of the Low Countries and the core of the later Dutch Republic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The dynasty began with Gerulf, a Frisian lord granted lands by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> in the 880s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirk-i-count-of-holland">Dirk I</a>, who received the land around Egmond in 922. The counts were first known as counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-frisia">West Frisia</a>, and the name Holland ('wood land') came into use around 1100.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts drained peat bogs, built dykes and founded towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dordrecht">Dordrecht</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirk-iii-count-of-holland">Dirk III</a> defeated an imperial army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vlaardingen">Vlaardingen</a> in 1018; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-holland">William II</a> was elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 1247 and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binnenhof">Binnenhof</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/floris-v-count-of-holland">Floris V</a> the towns, fishing and cloth trade grew rapidly, and water boards became a hallmark of local government.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Holland passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-avesnes">Avesnes</a> counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">Hainaut</a> in 1299, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbachs</a> in 1345 and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/burgundian-state">Burgundian State</a> in 1433, amid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hook-and-cod-wars">Hook and Cod wars</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Habsburg Netherlands</a> it led the revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-abjuration">Act of Abjuration</a> of 1581 it became the dominant province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-holland.jpg" alt="County of Holland" width="320" height="410"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_County_of_Holland_(1350).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>880 CE – 1581 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>The Hague</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dirk III, William II of Holland, Floris V</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Holland, County of West Frisia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1230&amp;focus=county-of-holland" aria-label="County of Holland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4777 378 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Holland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5097 634L5229 694M5097 634L5090 681M5097 634L5118 774M5097 634L5102 664M5097 634L5099 670M5097 634L5097 634M5097 634L5082 651"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5090 681h0M5118 774h0M5102 664h0M5099 670h0M5097 634h0M5082 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5097 63